window.BRENTWOOD_FULL_SIMULATIONS = [{"id":1,"name":"Full Simulation 01","reading":[{"cloze":{"text":"Urban trees provide benefits that extend beyond appearance. Their canopies abs___ solar radiation and red___ surface temperatures, while their roots help soil ret___ water after heavy rainfall. Trees can also filt___ some airborne pollutants and prov___ habitat for insects and birds. However, these benefits dep___ on species, location, and maintenance. A tree planted beneath power lines, for example, may req___ repeated pruning, whereas one placed near pavement may strug___ if its roots lack space. Effective urban forestry therefore inv___ not simply planting more trees, but selecting and managing them according to local cond___.","answers":[{"missing":"orb","word":"absorb"},{"missing":"uce","word":"reduce"},{"missing":"ain","word":"retain"},{"missing":"er","word":"filter"},{"missing":"ide","word":"provide"},{"missing":"end","word":"depend"},{"missing":"uire","word":"require"},{"missing":"gle","word":"struggle"},{"missing":"olve","word":"involve"},{"missing":"itions","word":"conditions"}]},"groups":[{"kind":"daily","questions":[{"n":11,"question":"Why will the fourth-floor study area close earlier?","options":{"A":"Fewer students are using it","B":"Equipment is being replaced","C":"Room 214 is being renovated","D":"The library is reducing its hours"},"answer":"B"},{"n":12,"question":"What is indicated about Room 214?","options":{"A":"It can be used for group meetings.","B":"It will close at the same time as the fourth floor.","C":"It provides an alternative for students who need quiet space.","D":"It will remain available only during the renovation."},"answer":"C"}],"stimulus":"Read a notice.\n\n### CENTRAL LIBRARY\n\nQuiet Floor Access Change\n\nBeginning Monday, the fourth-floor quiet study area will close at 8:00 p.m. instead of 10:00 p.m. while ventilation equipment is being replaced.\n\nStudents who need silent study space after 8:00 p.m. may use Room 214, which will remain open until midnight. Group work is not permitted in either location.\n\nThe project is expected to last approximately three weeks."},{"kind":"daily","questions":[{"n":13,"question":"What has changed about Thursday's meeting?","options":{"A":"Its starting time","B":"Its location","C":"Its purpose","D":"Its date"},"answer":"B"},{"n":14,"question":"Why does Dr. Mercer want to discuss the results earlier than planned?","options":{"A":"Several samples produced unexpected results.","B":"The laboratory equipment has been replaced.","C":"Some assistants have completed their work early.","D":"Another meeting has been scheduled for next month."},"answer":"A"},{"n":15,"question":"What can be inferred about assistants whose analysis is incomplete?","options":{"A":"They should finish it before attending.","B":"They should send their results instead of attending.","C":"They are still expected to attend the meeting.","D":"They will discuss their results next month."},"answer":"C"}],"stimulus":"Read an email.\n\nTo: researchassistants@northbridge.edu\nFrom: l.mercer@northbridge.edu\nSubject: Thursday Lab Meeting\n\nHello everyone,\n\nThursday's lab meeting will begin at the usual time, 2:00 p.m., but we will meet in Science 318 rather than Science 204 because new equipment is being installed in our regular room.\n\nPlease bring the preliminary results from last week's trial. I had originally planned to discuss them next month, but the unexpected variation in several samples makes an earlier review necessary.\n\nIf your results are not yet complete, bring whatever data you currently have. Do not delay your attendance simply to finish the analysis.\n\nBest,\nDr. Mercer"},{"kind":"academic","questions":[{"n":16,"question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":{"A":"Why birds from different species imitate one another","B":"How birdsong reflects both learning and biological constraints","C":"Why bird dialects disappear when populations migrate","D":"How adult birds deliberately teach songs to juveniles"},"answer":"B"},{"n":17,"question":"Why does the author mention neighboring populations?","options":{"A":"To show that members of the same species can develop different song traditions","B":"To explain why juvenile birds migrate before learning to sing","C":"To suggest that neighboring populations rarely communicate","D":"To demonstrate that song patterns are entirely genetic"},"answer":"A"},{"n":18,"question":"The word “constrain” is closest in meaning to","options":{"A":"record","B":"limit","C":"reveal","D":"imitate"},"answer":"B"},{"n":19,"question":"What can be inferred about a juvenile exposed to many different sounds?","options":{"A":"It will reproduce all of them equally well.","B":"It may still favor sounds typical of its own species.","C":"It will lose the ability to develop a recognizable song.","D":"It must learn directly from one particular adult."},"answer":"B"},{"n":20,"question":"Why are birdsongs useful to researchers according to the final paragraph?","options":{"A":"They change more quickly than all other learned behaviors.","B":"They allow researchers to study interactions between inherited and learned behavior.","C":"They reveal exactly when migration occurs.","D":"They demonstrate that cultural traditions are permanent."},"answer":"B"}],"stimulus":"### Cultural Transmission in Birdsong\n\nYoung birds of many species do not develop their songs entirely through instinct. Instead, they learn important features by listening to adults during a limited developmental period. Because juveniles often copy nearby individuals, song patterns can persist within a population even when neighboring populations of the same species sing somewhat differently.\n\nThese local differences are sometimes described as dialects. Their existence demonstrates that biological capacity alone does not determine the final form of a song. Yet cultural transmission is not unrestricted. A young bird exposed to songs from several species generally reproduces patterns characteristic of its own species, suggesting that inherited perceptual biases constrain what can be learned.\n\nDialects can also change. If individuals carrying unfamiliar song variants enter a population, their songs may gradually spread, disappear, or combine with existing forms. Consequently, birdsong provides researchers with a useful model for examining how inherited predispositions and socially acquired behavior interact."}]},{"cloze":{"text":"In markets where buyers cannot easily evaluate quality before making a purchase, reputation can bec___ economically important. Consumers may rel___ on previous experience, recommendations, or visible ratings when choo___ among sellers. These signals are imperfect, bec___ high ratings may reflect popularity rather than consistent quality. Nevertheless, sellers with established reputations often have an incen___ to maintain standards because poor performance can dam___ future demand. New sellers face a different problem: they may offer excellent products but lack the hist___ needed to establish trust. Some platforms attempt to red___ this disadvantage by verifying transactions or identifying unusually reliable reviewers. Such mechanisms do not elim___ uncertainty, but they may make information asymmetries less sev___.","answers":[{"missing":"ome","word":"become"},{"missing":"y","word":"rely"},{"missing":"sing","word":"choosing"},{"missing":"ause","word":"because"},{"missing":"tive","word":"incentive"},{"missing":"age","word":"damage"},{"missing":"ory","word":"history"},{"missing":"uce","word":"reduce"},{"missing":"inate","word":"eliminate"},{"missing":"ere","word":"severe"}]},"groups":[{"kind":"daily","questions":[{"n":11,"question":"Why can the bicycle not be returned to Station C?","options":{"A":"The station is closed.","B":"The station has no available spaces.","C":"The reservation was made at another station.","D":"The bicycle requires maintenance."},"answer":"B"},{"n":12,"question":"Under what condition will additional time be provided automatically?","options":{"A":"The bicycle is returned after 5:20 p.m.","B":"Station C becomes available again.","C":"Both suggested return stations become full.","D":"The user reserves another bicycle."},"answer":"C"}],"stimulus":"Read a message.\n\nCampus Bike Share\n\nYour bicycle reservation for Station C expires at 5:20 p.m.\n\nBecause Station C is currently full, bicycles cannot be returned there. Please return your bicycle to either Station B or Station D. Returning it after 5:20 p.m. will result in an additional usage charge.\n\nIf both alternative stations become full, the app will automatically extend your reservation by 15 minutes."},{"kind":"daily","questions":[{"n":13,"question":"Why should audience members use the east courtyard entrance?","options":{"A":"The main entrance is undergoing repairs.","B":"The main lobby will close before the performance.","C":"Printed tickets are available only there.","D":"The theater has moved the performance."},"answer":"A"},{"n":14,"question":"What is indicated about Ms. Patel's tickets?","options":{"A":"They must be exchanged at the box office.","B":"They can still be used in their current form.","C":"They are valid only if she arrives early.","D":"They were issued for the wrong entrance."},"answer":"B"},{"n":15,"question":"Why does the theater recommend arriving early?","options":{"A":"Seats will be assigned when audience members arrive.","B":"Ticket collection may take additional time.","C":"Entry may proceed more slowly than usual.","D":"The performance could begin ahead of schedule."},"answer":"C"}],"stimulus":"Read an email.\n\nTo: n.patel@dmail.com\nFrom: riverstreettheater@dmail.com\nSubject: Saturday Performance\n\nDear Ms. Patel,\n\nThank you for purchasing two tickets for Saturday's 7:30 p.m. performance of The Long Winter.\n\nBecause repairs to the main entrance are taking longer than anticipated, audience members should enter through the east courtyard entrance on Mason Street. The box office inside the main lobby will remain accessible, but only from inside the building.\n\nYour digital tickets remain valid, so there is no need to collect printed copies. We recommend arriving at least twenty minutes early because the temporary entrance is narrower than the main entrance.\n\nSincerely,\nRiver Street Theater"},{"kind":"academic","questions":[{"n":16,"question":"What is the main purpose of the passage?","options":{"A":"To explain why useful innovations may nevertheless spread gradually","B":"To argue that consumers usually misunderstand new technologies","C":"To compare electric vehicles with communication platforms","D":"To demonstrate that technical improvements reduce uncertainty"},"answer":"A"},{"n":17,"question":"What does the electric-vehicle example illustrate?","options":{"A":"Consumers prefer technologies requiring little infrastructure.","B":"An innovation's usefulness can depend on supporting systems.","C":"Network effects occur only in transportation markets.","D":"Infrastructure is usually developed before new technologies appear."},"answer":"B"},{"n":18,"question":"The phrase “network effects” refers to a situation in which","options":{"A":"a product becomes less costly as production rises","B":"a technology's value is partly affected by how many others use it","C":"consumers receive information through advertising networks","D":"users adopt products because technical quality declines"},"answer":"B"},{"n":19,"question":"Why might potential adopters delay a decision?","options":{"A":"They may be uncertain about benefits that are difficult to evaluate.","B":"They may be unaware that competing technologies exist.","C":"They may prefer immediate costs to future costs.","D":"They may believe that useful innovations always disappear quickly."},"answer":"A"},{"n":20,"question":"What can be inferred from the final paragraph?","options":{"A":"Delayed adoption can sometimes be a reasonable decision.","B":"Consumers eventually adopt every technically superior innovation.","C":"Waiting always reduces the cost of a new technology.","D":"Infrastructure has little effect after an innovation is introduced."},"answer":"A"}],"stimulus":"### Why Some Innovations Spread Slowly\n\nAn innovation can be demonstrably useful without being adopted immediately. Economists and sociologists have long noted that individuals rarely evaluate new technologies solely by comparing their technical advantages with those of existing alternatives.\n\nAdoption may depend on complementary systems. An electric vehicle, for instance, becomes more practical when charging infrastructure is widely available. Similarly, a communication platform has limited value if few of the people with whom a user wishes to communicate have joined it. Such technologies exhibit network effects: their usefulness partly depends on the behavior of other users.\n\nUncertainty also matters. Potential adopters may postpone a decision when costs are immediate but benefits are unfamiliar or difficult to estimate. Consequently, slow adoption does not necessarily imply that people have failed to recognize an innovation's advantages. It may instead reflect the absence of supporting infrastructure, uncertainty about future benefits, or a rational preference to wait until other users have adopted first."}]}],"listening":[{"choose":[{"n":1,"prompt":"Woman: Didn't Maya say she'd send the revised schedule today?","options":{"A":"I thought she already had.","B":"The schedule lasted three hours.","C":"Yes, Maya revised it yesterday.","D":"I'll schedule the meeting downstairs."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m1-response-01.mp3"},{"n":2,"prompt":"Man: Would you mind closing the window?","options":{"A":"It was closer than I expected.","B":"Not at all.","C":"I haven't found it yet.","D":"The weather was mild."},"answer":"B","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m1-response-02.mp3"},{"n":3,"prompt":"Woman: Why hasn't the package arrived?","options":{"A":"Apparently, it was sent to the wrong building.","B":"Yes, I arrived early.","C":"The package is rather small.","D":"I'd prefer the other one."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m1-response-03.mp3"},{"n":4,"prompt":"Man: I thought the seminar was supposed to end at four.","options":{"A":"So did I.","B":"It ended successfully.","C":"Four people attended.","D":"I suppose the seminar room is available."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m1-response-04.mp3"},{"n":5,"prompt":"Woman: Are there any seats left near the front?","options":{"A":"I left my bag there.","B":"Just a couple, I think.","C":"The lecture was about seating design.","D":"Yes, the front door is open."},"answer":"B","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m1-response-05.mp3"},{"n":6,"prompt":"Man: How come you didn't submit the form online?","options":{"A":"The website kept rejecting it.","B":"I formed the group yesterday.","C":"Yes, online courses are convenient.","D":"It should be submitted tomorrow."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m1-response-06.mp3"},{"n":7,"prompt":"Woman: You haven't seen my notebook, have you?","options":{"A":"I took several notes.","B":"No, but I'll keep an eye out for it.","C":"The notebook is very useful.","D":"Yes, I haven't."},"answer":"B","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m1-response-07.mp3"},{"n":8,"prompt":"Man: Could we postpone the discussion until tomorrow?","options":{"A":"Tomorrow's report was interesting.","B":"That's what we discussed.","C":"Sure, if everyone else agrees.","D":"It was postponed yesterday."},"answer":"C","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m1-response-08.mp3"}],"groups":[{"kind":"conversation-1","title":"Conversation 1","transcript":"Listen once.\n\nStudent: Hi. I received an email saying my statistics tutorial was moved to Wednesday, but my schedule still shows Tuesday.\n\nStaff member: The department changed several tutorial groups yesterday. The online schedule won't update until tonight.\n\nStudent: So I should come Wednesday?\n\nStaff member: Yes. Same time, different day. Your classroom hasn't changed.\n\nStudent: Okay. I was worried I'd been moved to another section entirely.","questions":[{"n":9,"question":"Why did the student speak with the staff member?","options":{"A":"To change tutorial groups","B":"To confirm when a tutorial will occur","C":"To report an incorrect classroom","D":"To request access to the online schedule"},"answer":"B"},{"n":10,"question":"What does the staff member indicate?","options":{"A":"The student's classroom will change tonight.","B":"The student was moved into another course.","C":"The online schedule has not yet been updated.","D":"The Tuesday tutorial will continue as usual."},"answer":"C"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m1-01-conversation-1.mp3"},{"kind":"conversation-2","title":"Conversation 2","transcript":"Student: I wanted to reserve one of the photography studios for Friday afternoon, but the website shows all of them as unavailable.\n\nEmployee: That's because the department is inspecting the lighting equipment Friday morning. We blocked the entire day in case the inspection runs late.\n\nStudent: So there's no chance of using one?\n\nEmployee: Check the site after noon. If the inspection finishes on time, we'll release the afternoon reservations.","questions":[{"n":11,"question":"Why are the studios currently unavailable for Friday?","options":{"A":"Students have already reserved them.","B":"New lighting equipment is being installed.","C":"The department does not yet know when an inspection will finish.","D":"The reservation website is malfunctioning."},"answer":"C"},{"n":12,"question":"What does the employee suggest the student do?","options":{"A":"Make a reservation for another department","B":"Return to the studio Friday morning","C":"Check for availability later","D":"Contact the equipment inspector"},"answer":"C"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m1-02-conversation-2.mp3"},{"kind":"announcement","title":"Announcement","transcript":"Listen once.\n\nGood afternoon. Students planning to attend tomorrow's outdoor film screening should note that the event has been moved from West Lawn to the Student Union auditorium because rain is expected during the evening. The screening will still begin at eight o'clock. Doors will open at seven-thirty, and tickets already issued for the outdoor event remain valid. Because seating in the auditorium is limited, students who have not yet reserved a ticket should not come expecting admission at the door.","questions":[{"n":13,"question":"Why has the event been moved?","options":{"A":"More students registered than expected.","B":"The outdoor equipment is unavailable.","C":"Poor weather is expected.","D":"The film requires special projection equipment."},"answer":"C"},{"n":14,"question":"What are students without reservations advised to do?","options":{"A":"Arrive before 7:30","B":"Avoid relying on tickets being available at the entrance","C":"Exchange their outdoor tickets","D":"Contact the Student Union for another screening"},"answer":"B"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m1-03-announcement.mp3"},{"kind":"academic-talk","title":"Academic Talk","transcript":"Listen once.\n\nWhen people think about plant competition, they often imagine plants competing for sunlight above the ground. But substantial competition also occurs below the surface. Roots from neighboring plants may occupy the same region of soil and draw upon limited supplies of water and nutrients.\n\nInterestingly, plants do not always respond to neighboring roots simply by producing more roots. In some experiments, a plant produces fewer roots when the neighboring plant is genetically related to it than when the neighbor is unrelated. Researchers have proposed that reducing competition among relatives may increase the survival of closely related individuals.\n\nHowever, interpreting these experiments is difficult. Root growth is influenced by soil chemistry, moisture, container size, and many other variables. Consequently, researchers must distinguish responses specifically caused by neighboring plants from responses caused by changes those neighbors produce in the soil.","questions":[{"n":15,"question":"What is the talk mainly about?","options":{"A":"Methods for increasing agricultural production","B":"How plants may respond to below-ground competition","C":"Why roots grow faster than other plant structures","D":"How soil chemistry determines genetic relationships"},"answer":"B"},{"n":16,"question":"What has been observed in some experiments involving related plants?","options":{"A":"They may produce fewer competing roots near one another.","B":"They absorb substantially more water than unrelated plants.","C":"They grow only when placed in separate containers.","D":"They change the chemical composition of their roots."},"answer":"A"},{"n":17,"question":"Why might reduced competition among relatives be beneficial?","options":{"A":"It could increase the survival of genetically related individuals.","B":"It prevents all changes in soil chemistry.","C":"It causes roots to occupy a larger area.","D":"It eliminates competition for sunlight."},"answer":"A"},{"n":18,"question":"Why does the speaker mention soil chemistry and moisture?","options":{"A":"To describe how researchers identify plant species","B":"To argue that root experiments should take place outdoors","C":"To illustrate factors that can complicate interpretation of results","D":"To explain why related plants require different nutrients"},"answer":"C"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m1-04-academic-talk.mp3"}]},{"choose":[{"n":1,"prompt":"Man: Haven't they finished repairing the elevator yet?","options":{"A":"Not as far as I know.","B":"I finished upstairs.","C":"The repairman uses the elevator.","D":"Yes, the stairs are nearby."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m2-response-01.mp3"},{"n":2,"prompt":"Woman: What made you change your research topic?","options":{"A":"The original question turned out to be too broad.","B":"I researched it several times.","C":"The change was announced yesterday.","D":"My topic begins on page four."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m2-response-02.mp3"},{"n":3,"prompt":"Man: I could barely hear the speaker from the back.","options":{"A":"Neither could I.","B":"The speaker came from Boston.","C":"I'll speak with her later.","D":"The back entrance is closed."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m2-response-03.mp3"},{"n":4,"prompt":"Woman: Do you happen to know whether this bus stops near the museum?","options":{"A":"The exhibit opened last week.","B":"I happened to miss it.","C":"I think the next stop is the closest one.","D":"No, the bus wasn't late."},"answer":"C","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m2-response-04.mp3"},{"n":5,"prompt":"Man: Wasn't Leo supposed to lead today's meeting?","options":{"A":"He asked Priya to take over.","B":"The meeting was rather productive.","C":"Yes, Leo attended last week.","D":"It's supposed to be upstairs."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m2-response-05.mp3"},{"n":6,"prompt":"Woman: How did you manage to finish so early?","options":{"A":"I started the analysis yesterday.","B":"The finish was surprisingly smooth.","C":"It was managed by another department.","D":"Early classes are difficult."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m2-response-06.mp3"},{"n":7,"prompt":"Man: I wonder if they'll extend the application deadline.","options":{"A":"I already submitted mine, just in case.","B":"The application was fairly long.","C":"The extension is on the second floor.","D":"Yes, I wondered about the applicant."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m2-response-07.mp3"},{"n":8,"prompt":"Woman: You wouldn't happen to have an extra charger, would you?","options":{"A":"The battery lasts several hours.","B":"Actually, I think there's one in my bag.","C":"I wouldn't charge you for it.","D":"The extra class begins tomorrow."},"answer":"B","audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m2-response-08.mp3"}],"groups":[{"kind":"conversation","title":"Conversation","transcript":"Student: Professor Harris, could I ask about the dataset for our assignment? Some entries are blank, and I wasn't sure whether we were supposed to remove them.\n\nProfessor: Don't remove anything yet. Those missing values are intentional.\n\nStudent: We're supposed to decide how to handle them?\n\nProfessor: Exactly. Part of the assignment is explaining why you chose a particular method. There isn't one solution that works equally well in every case.","questions":[{"n":9,"question":"Why does the student speak to the professor?","options":{"A":"To request a different dataset","B":"To ask how to interpret an assignment requirement","C":"To report that data were accidentally deleted","D":"To explain why an assignment is incomplete"},"answer":"B"},{"n":10,"question":"What does the professor imply about the missing values?","options":{"A":"Students are expected to make a methodological decision about them.","B":"They should all be replaced with the same value.","C":"They were accidentally omitted from the dataset.","D":"They will not affect the assignment."},"answer":"A"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m2-01-conversation.mp3"},{"kind":"announcement","title":"Announcement","transcript":"Students registered for next week's career fair should check their confirmation messages before arriving. Companies have been assigned to two different halls, and the location printed on early registration confirmations may no longer be accurate. Your updated message identifies the correct hall and includes a QR code required for entry. If you cannot locate the message, visit the registration desk in the main lobby rather than joining either entrance line.","questions":[{"n":11,"question":"Why should students check their confirmation messages?","options":{"A":"The time of the fair has changed.","B":"Some companies canceled their participation.","C":"Their assigned location may have changed.","D":"They may need to register again."},"answer":"C"},{"n":12,"question":"What should a student do if the updated message cannot be found?","options":{"A":"Enter whichever hall has the shorter line","B":"Go to the registration desk","C":"Request a QR code from a company representative","D":"Return at a later time"},"answer":"B"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m2-02-announcement.mp3"},{"kind":"academic-talk","title":"Academic Talk","transcript":"Many archaeological objects survive not because the materials from which they were made are naturally durable, but because the environments in which they were deposited slowed their deterioration.\n\nWood, for example, normally decays when microorganisms consume its organic material. Yet wooden objects can survive for centuries in waterlogged soil because saturated conditions contain very little oxygen, restricting many of the organisms responsible for decomposition.\n\nParadoxically, excavation may place such objects in greater danger. Once waterlogged wood is exposed to air, evaporation removes the water supporting its weakened cellular structure. If the object simply dries, it may shrink, crack, or collapse.\n\nConservators therefore replace the water gradually with substances that support the structure as drying occurs. Preservation, in other words, does not end when an artifact is removed from the ground. Excavation can initiate an entirely new phase of deterioration unless environmental changes are carefully controlled.","questions":[{"n":13,"question":"What is the main purpose of the talk?","options":{"A":"To explain why excavation can threaten some preserved artifacts","B":"To compare wooden artifacts from different archaeological periods","C":"To describe methods used to locate artifacts in wet environments","D":"To argue that water permanently prevents organic materials from decaying"},"answer":"A"},{"n":14,"question":"Why can wood survive in waterlogged soil?","options":{"A":"Water strengthens its cellular structure.","B":"Low oxygen limits many organisms that cause decay.","C":"Minerals in the soil replace the organic material.","D":"Evaporation occurs unusually slowly underground."},"answer":"B"},{"n":15,"question":"What happens when waterlogged wood dries too quickly?","options":{"A":"Its original color becomes impossible to identify.","B":"Microorganisms immediately consume it.","C":"Its weakened structure may deform or collapse.","D":"Oxygen is removed from its cells."},"answer":"C"},{"n":16,"question":"What point does the speaker make in the final paragraph?","options":{"A":"Excavation is normally unnecessary when artifacts are well preserved.","B":"Archaeological preservation may require continued intervention after excavation.","C":"Conservators should avoid replacing water inside artifacts.","D":"Environmental changes rarely affect excavated objects."},"answer":"B"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-01/listening/m2-03-academic-talk.mp3"}]}],"writing":{"build":[{"n":1,"question":"Why hasn't Mina submitted the application?","prefix":"She","suffix":".","tokens":["hasn't","decided","which program","to apply","to"],"correctOrder":["hasn't","decided","which program","to apply","to"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"She hasn't decided which program to apply to."},{"n":2,"question":"Did the professor explain the revised deadline?","prefix":"Yes, but","suffix":".","tokens":["I","didn't understand","why","it had been","changed","initially"],"correctOrder":["I","didn't understand","why","it had been","changed","initially"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"Yes, but I didn't understand why it had been changed initially."},{"n":3,"question":"Can we use the laboratory this afternoon?","prefix":"Only if","suffix":".","tokens":["the technician","has finished","repairing","the ventilation","system"],"correctOrder":["the technician","has finished","repairing","the ventilation","system"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"Only if the technician has finished repairing the ventilation system."},{"n":4,"question":"Why are you reading the report again?","prefix":"I'm","suffix":".","tokens":["trying","to determine","whether","the conclusion","is supported","by the data"],"correctOrder":["trying","to determine","whether","the conclusion","is supported","by the data"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"I'm trying to determine whether the conclusion is supported by the data."},{"n":5,"question":"Was the conference useful?","prefix":"It was","suffix":".","tokens":["much more","informative","than","I","had","expected","rarely"],"correctOrder":["much more","informative","than","I","had","expected"],"unused":["rarely"],"correctSentence":"It was much more informative than I had expected."},{"n":6,"question":"When did the researchers notice the inconsistency?","prefix":"Only after","suffix":".","tokens":["the results","had been","compared","across","groups"],"correctOrder":["the results","had been","compared","across","groups"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"Only after the results had been compared across groups."},{"n":7,"question":"Are you going to accept the internship?","prefix":"That depends on","suffix":".","tokens":["whether","the schedule","can be","adjusted","slightly"],"correctOrder":["whether","the schedule","can be","adjusted","slightly"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"That depends on whether the schedule can be adjusted slightly."},{"n":8,"question":"Why did Sam contact the financial-aid office?","prefix":"He wanted to know","suffix":".","tokens":["whether","his scholarship","would still","apply","if","he transferred"],"correctOrder":["whether","his scholarship","would still","apply","if","he transferred"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"He wanted to know whether his scholarship would still apply if he transferred."},{"n":9,"question":"Did the committee approve the proposal immediately?","prefix":"No. Not until","suffix":".","tokens":["several revisions","had been made","did","they","approve it"],"correctOrder":["several revisions","had been made","did","they","approve it"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"Not until several revisions had been made did they approve it."},{"n":10,"question":"How did you prepare for the presentation?","prefix":"I used","suffix":".","tokens":["the outline","that","had been prepared","by","my supervisor","it"],"correctOrder":["the outline","that","had been prepared","by","my supervisor"],"unused":["it"],"correctSentence":"I used the outline that had been prepared by my supervisor."}],"email":"Time: 7 minutes\n\nYou registered for a weekend photography workshop because participants were supposed to have access to professional lighting equipment.\n\nYou have now received a message saying that the equipment will not be available. Participants may still attend, but they must bring their own equipment.\n\nWrite an email to Jordan Lee, the workshop coordinator.\n\nIn your email:\n\n- Explain why access to the equipment was important to you.\n- Describe the problem the change creates.\n- Ask about a possible solution or alternative.\n\nWrite as much as you can and in complete sentences.\n\nTo: Jordan Lee\nSubject: Photography Workshop Equipment","discussion":"Time: 10 minutes\n\nYour professor is teaching a class on education.\n\n### Professor Adler\n\nSome universities record lectures and allow students to watch them later. Supporters argue that recordings help students review difficult material and accommodate unavoidable absences. Others worry that easy access to recordings may reduce attendance and classroom participation.\n\nShould universities routinely make recordings of lectures available to students? Why or why not?\n\n### Serena\n\nI think recordings should normally be available. Students sometimes miss information even when they attend class, especially when a lecture introduces difficult concepts. Being able to review part of the lecture could improve understanding.\n\n### Marcus\n\nI would limit access to recordings. If students know every lecture will be available later, some may stop attending regularly. Classroom questions and discussions are also important parts of learning and cannot always be reproduced by a recording.\n\n### Your Response\n\nWrite at least 100 words."},"speaking":{"repeat":{"scenario":"You are learning how to assist visitors at a university science museum. Listen to your supervisor and repeat exactly what she says. Repeat only once.","prompts":["Welcome to the university science museum.","The information desk is beside the entrance.","Temporary exhibits are located on the second floor.","Visitors may leave their coats and bags in these lockers.","Guided tours begin every hour near the central staircase.","If an exhibit is temporarily closed, please direct visitors toward another gallery nearby.","Before each tour begins, remind visitors that food and uncovered drinks are not permitted inside the exhibition rooms."],"audio":["/media/audio/sim-01/speaking/repeat-01.mp3","/media/audio/sim-01/speaking/repeat-02.mp3","/media/audio/sim-01/speaking/repeat-03.mp3","/media/audio/sim-01/speaking/repeat-04.mp3","/media/audio/sim-01/speaking/repeat-05.mp3","/media/audio/sim-01/speaking/repeat-06.mp3","/media/audio/sim-01/speaking/repeat-07.mp3"]},"interview":{"scenario":"You have volunteered for a university research study about how students choose places to study. A researcher will ask you four questions.","questions":["Where do you usually prefer to study when you need to concentrate, and why?","Some students prefer complete silence, while others prefer background noise. Which environment helps you work more effectively?","Imagine that your university could improve either its library study areas or its online study resources, but not both this year. Which improvement should it prioritize, and why?","Some people believe physical study spaces will become less important as digital learning expands. How might this change benefit students, and what problem could it create?"],"audio":["/media/audio/sim-01/speaking/interview-01.mp3","/media/audio/sim-01/speaking/interview-02.mp3","/media/audio/sim-01/speaking/interview-03.mp3","/media/audio/sim-01/speaking/interview-04.mp3"]}}},{"id":2,"name":"Full Simulation 02","reading":[{"cloze":{"text":"Mangrove forests occupy coastlines where few tree species can survive. Their roots tol___ salty water and help tra___ sediment carried by tides. As sediment accumulates, shorelines may bec___ more stable and less vul___ to erosion. Mangroves also prov___ habitat for young fish and other organisms. Yet restoring these forests is not simply a matter of pla___ seedlings. Changes in water flow can prev___ roots from receiving enough oxygen, while inappropriate species selection may red___ survival. Successful restoration therefore req___ careful attention to local hydrology rather than merely increasing the num___ of trees.","answers":[{"missing":"erate","word":"tolerate"},{"missing":"p","word":"trap"},{"missing":"ome","word":"become"},{"missing":"nerable","word":"vulnerable"},{"missing":"ide","word":"provide"},{"missing":"nting","word":"planting"},{"missing":"ent","word":"prevent"},{"missing":"uce","word":"reduce"},{"missing":"ires","word":"requires"},{"missing":"ber","word":"number"}]},"groups":[{"kind":"daily","questions":[{"n":11,"question":"Who must complete the online refresher?","options":{"A":"Everyone who enters the makerspace","B":"Students who need renewed authorization for laser cutters","C":"Only students making new reservations","D":"Students who completed the full course this semester"},"answer":"B"},{"n":12,"question":"What will happen to an existing reservation if the refresher has not been completed?","options":{"A":"It will automatically be canceled.","B":"It will be moved to another machine.","C":"The reservation will remain, but the machine cannot be used.","D":"The full safety course must be taken again."},"answer":"C"}],"stimulus":"Read a notice.\n\n### ENGINEERING MAKERSPACE\n\nBeginning September 8, students must complete a 15-minute online safety refresher before reserving laser cutters.\n\nStudents who completed the full safety course this semester do not need to repeat the course. The refresher will automatically appear in their online accounts when required.\n\nExisting reservations will remain valid, but access to the machine will be blocked until the refresher has been completed."},{"kind":"daily","questions":[{"n":13,"question":"Why will the washing machines be unavailable?","options":{"A":"New machines are being delivered.","B":"Part of the building's water system is being repaired.","C":"The laundry room is being moved.","D":"Student access cards are being replaced."},"answer":"B"},{"n":14,"question":"What can residents still do in Laundry Room 3B during the work?","options":{"A":"Dry clothes","B":"Wash small loads","C":"Update access cards","D":"Use machines from another floor"},"answer":"A"},{"n":15,"question":"Why might a resident need to visit the front desk?","options":{"A":"To reserve a washing machine","B":"To report a damaged dryer","C":"To obtain access to one of the alternative laundry rooms","D":"To receive reimbursement for laundry costs"},"answer":"C"}],"stimulus":"Read an email.\n\nTo: residence.students@fairmont.edu\nFrom: housing@fairmont.edu\nSubject: Laundry Room 3B\n\nHello,\n\nThe washing machines in Laundry Room 3B will be unavailable on Tuesday from 9:00 a.m. until approximately 2:00 p.m. while technicians replace the building's water-control valves.\n\nThe dryers will remain operational, but because no washing machines will be available, residents may use the laundry rooms on floors 1 and 5 during the work.\n\nPlease note that cards issued before August may not open the fifth-floor laundry room. If your card does not work, the front desk can update it immediately.\n\nHousing Services"},{"kind":"academic","questions":[{"n":16,"question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":{"A":"Why digital devices permanently reduce human memory","B":"How people use external resources to reduce memory demands","C":"Why written reminders are more effective than digital reminders","D":"How computer storage increases the amount of information people learn"},"answer":"B"},{"n":17,"question":"Why does the author mention leaving an object near the door?","options":{"A":"To illustrate an everyday form of cognitive offloading","B":"To show that physical reminders outperform electronic ones","C":"To explain why spatial memory develops early","D":"To describe a technique used in an experiment"},"answer":"A"},{"n":18,"question":"What happened to participants who expected information to remain available?","options":{"A":"They remembered more details and fewer locations.","B":"They remembered fewer details but more about where information was stored.","C":"They were unable to locate the information afterward.","D":"They performed identically to the other participants."},"answer":"B"},{"n":19,"question":"The word “adaptively” is closest in meaning to","options":{"A":"in a way that adjusts to circumstances","B":"without conscious awareness","C":"with increasing difficulty","D":"according to fixed biological rules"},"answer":"A"},{"n":20,"question":"What does the final paragraph suggest?","options":{"A":"Forgetting information always indicates poorer memory ability.","B":"External tools eliminate the need for memory.","C":"Changes in recall may reflect a shift in what information people prioritize.","D":"Location memory is generally more accurate than factual memory."},"answer":"C"}],"stimulus":"### Cognitive Offloading\n\nPeople often reduce the demands placed on memory by storing information outside the mind. Writing a shopping list, setting a phone reminder, or leaving an object near the door as a cue are examples of cognitive offloading.\n\nSuch strategies can improve performance because they allow limited mental resources to be used elsewhere. However, relying on external aids may also alter what people remember. In one experiment, participants who expected information to remain accessible on a computer recalled fewer details than participants who believed the information would later disappear. Yet the first group was better at remembering where the information had been stored.\n\nThis does not necessarily mean that digital tools weaken memory. Instead, people may adaptively redistribute what they try to remember. When information can easily be retrieved, remembering its location may become more useful than memorizing the information itself. Researchers therefore distinguish between a reduction in memory performance and a change in what memory is being used for."}]},{"cloze":{"text":"Archaeologists rarely interpret an artifact in isolation. An object's mea___ often depends on where it was found and what surrounded it. A ceramic vessel discovered inside a burial, for example, may have ser___ a different purpose from an apparently similar vessel found in a household kitchen. The surrounding materials can also rev___ whether an object was discarded accidentally, deposited deliberately, or moved after its ori___ use. For this reason, excavation requires met___ documentation. Removing an artifact without recording its exact position can des___ information that cannot later be recovered. Modern archaeological practice therefore tre___ context as evidence in its own right, rather than as a sec___ detail. Even an ordinary object may become highly inf___ when its relationship to other materials is car___ recorded.","answers":[{"missing":"ning","word":"meaning"},{"missing":"ved","word":"served"},{"missing":"eal","word":"reveal"},{"missing":"ginal","word":"original"},{"missing":"iculous","word":"meticulous"},{"missing":"troy","word":"destroy"},{"missing":"ats","word":"treats"},{"missing":"ondary","word":"secondary"},{"missing":"ormative","word":"informative"},{"missing":"efully","word":"carefully"}]},"groups":[{"kind":"daily","questions":[{"n":11,"question":"Why is the Green Route being changed?","options":{"A":"University Hall is closed.","B":"The route is being permanently redesigned.","C":"Work will prevent buses from using part of the usual route.","D":"The Blue Route needs additional space."},"answer":"C"},{"n":12,"question":"What is indicated about passengers who normally use the Green Route?","options":{"A":"They can board nearby at a temporary location.","B":"They must use the Blue Route instead.","C":"They should expect the change to last all week.","D":"They cannot travel to University Hall."},"answer":"A"}],"stimulus":"Read a notice.\n\n### STUDENT TRANSIT UPDATE\n\nThe Green Route will not stop at University Hall from Monday through Wednesday because College Avenue will be closed for utility work.\n\nDuring this period, Green Route passengers should use the temporary stop on Baker Street, approximately three minutes on foot from University Hall.\n\nThe Blue Route is unaffected and will continue using its regular University Hall stop."},{"kind":"daily","questions":[{"n":13,"question":"What are interns told NOT to do?","options":{"A":"Print the handbook themselves","B":"Bring an electronic device","C":"Attend the afternoon activity","D":"Use the training site during orientation"},"answer":"A"},{"n":14,"question":"Why can interns not access the training site before Monday?","options":{"A":"The site is currently being repaired.","B":"Their login information has not yet been provided.","C":"The activity has not been uploaded.","D":"Access is limited to staff computers."},"answer":"B"},{"n":15,"question":"What should an intern do if a laptop or tablet is unavailable?","options":{"A":"Complete the activity after orientation","B":"Borrow one from another intern","C":"Contact the program in advance","D":"Use a printed version of the training site"},"answer":"C"}],"stimulus":"Read an email.\n\nTo: summerinterns@hartwell.org\nFrom: programs@hartwell.org\nSubject: Orientation Materials\n\nDear Interns,\n\nYour orientation begins Monday at 9:00 a.m. in Conference Room A.\n\nYou do not need to print the orientation handbook. A printed copy will be provided when you arrive. Please bring a laptop or tablet, however, because one afternoon activity requires access to the organization's internal training site.\n\nLogin credentials for that site will be distributed during orientation and will not work before Monday.\n\nIf you cannot bring a device, reply to this message by Friday so that we can reserve one for you.\n\nBest,\nInternship Programs"},{"kind":"academic","questions":[{"n":16,"question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":{"A":"Why efficient technologies always replace older technologies","B":"How previous choices can influence later economic outcomes","C":"Why businesses frequently change technological systems","D":"How suppliers determine which technologies are invented"},"answer":"B"},{"n":17,"question":"Why does the author describe workers learning to use a technology?","options":{"A":"To illustrate how previous adoption can make switching more costly","B":"To show that training is the main source of technological innovation","C":"To explain why workers usually oppose efficient technologies","D":"To demonstrate that competing technologies require identical skills"},"answer":"A"},{"n":18,"question":"The word “dominant” is closest in meaning to","options":{"A":"expensive","B":"complex","C":"widely established","D":"newly developed"},"answer":"C"},{"n":19,"question":"Which statement would the author most likely agree with?","options":{"A":"Early adoption can sometimes affect the later success of a technology.","B":"Historical circumstances are more important than efficiency in every case.","C":"Once adopted, a technology can never be replaced.","D":"Businesses rarely consider the cost of switching technologies."},"answer":"A"},{"n":20,"question":"Why might comparing only current technical merits be misleading?","options":{"A":"Technical performance cannot be measured accurately.","B":"Current adoption may also reflect accumulated effects of earlier decisions.","C":"Older technologies are usually more efficient.","D":"Suppliers frequently hide information from consumers."},"answer":"B"}],"stimulus":"### Path Dependence\n\nSome economic outcomes are influenced not only by current advantages but also by decisions made much earlier. Economists describe this phenomenon as path dependence.\n\nConsider a technology that initially gains users because it happens to be available slightly earlier than a competing technology. Once businesses purchase compatible equipment, workers learn to use it, and suppliers begin supporting it, switching becomes increasingly costly. The technology may therefore remain dominant even if an alternative later becomes somewhat more efficient.\n\nPath dependence does not imply that historical accidents determine every outcome permanently. A sufficiently large improvement can still cause users to switch. Rather, the concept emphasizes that present choices are made within systems partly created by previous choices. Consequently, comparing two technologies only on their current technical merits may fail to explain why one has become widespread while the other has not."}]}],"listening":[{"choose":[{"n":1,"prompt":"Woman: Weren't you going to meet Daniel after class?","options":{"A":"I was, but he had to leave early.","B":"Daniel's class meets twice a week.","C":"Yes, I went to class.","D":"I don't know what he teaches."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m1-response-01.mp3"},{"n":2,"prompt":"Man: Do you know where I can get this form signed?","options":{"A":"I signed up yesterday.","B":"The department office should be able to help.","C":"The form was surprisingly long.","D":"No, my signature hasn't changed."},"answer":"B","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m1-response-02.mp3"},{"n":3,"prompt":"Woman: Why did they postpone the field trip?","options":{"A":"The weather forecast changed.","B":"Yes, the field is nearby.","C":"I postponed my assignment too.","D":"About thirty students attended."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m1-response-03.mp3"},{"n":4,"prompt":"Man: I can't believe the printer is broken again.","options":{"A":"Neither can I.","B":"I printed three copies.","C":"The printer is beside the window.","D":"Yes, I believe the article."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m1-response-04.mp3"},{"n":5,"prompt":"Woman: Would you be able to cover my shift Friday morning?","options":{"A":"The cover is on the table.","B":"I think so, but let me check my schedule.","C":"Friday's meeting was canceled.","D":"I worked there last year."},"answer":"B","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m1-response-05.mp3"},{"n":6,"prompt":"Man: How did your presentation go?","options":{"A":"Better than I expected.","B":"It went to the second floor.","C":"I presented the document yesterday.","D":"About fifteen minutes ago."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m1-response-06.mp3"},{"n":7,"prompt":"Woman: You didn't happen to save a copy of that file, did you?","options":{"A":"Actually, I think I did.","B":"The file cabinet is locked.","C":"No, it wasn't very expensive.","D":"I saved enough money."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m1-response-07.mp3"},{"n":8,"prompt":"Man: Shouldn't we reserve the room before we announce the meeting?","options":{"A":"That's probably a good idea.","B":"The announcement was yesterday.","C":"I don't think the room was large.","D":"We met there last semester."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m1-response-08.mp3"}],"groups":[{"kind":"conversation-1","title":"Conversation 1","transcript":"Student: Hi. I got a message saying the microscope I reserved is ready for pickup, but I thought my reservation was for tomorrow.\n\nLab assistant: It is. The message just means we've prepared the equipment. You don't need to collect it today.\n\nStudent: Oh, good. I was worried the reservation time had changed.\n\nLab assistant: No. Come tomorrow at ten, as scheduled. Just remember that you'll need your lab card to take the microscope out.","questions":[{"n":9,"question":"Why did the student speak to the lab assistant?","options":{"A":"To change a reservation time","B":"To clarify a message about reserved equipment","C":"To report that a microscope was missing","D":"To request a new laboratory card"},"answer":"B"},{"n":10,"question":"What does the lab assistant remind the student to do?","options":{"A":"Return the microscope today","B":"Bring identification required for borrowing the equipment","C":"Confirm the reservation online","D":"Arrive earlier than originally scheduled"},"answer":"B"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m1-01-conversation-1.mp3"},{"kind":"conversation-2","title":"Conversation 2","transcript":"Student: Professor Kim, I tried to add your environmental-policy course, but the registration system says I need permission.\n\nProfessor: That's because you're not in the public-policy program, right?\n\nStudent: Right. I'm an economics major.\n\nProfessor: That's fine. The restriction is there because policy students register first. There are still several seats, so I'll approve your request this afternoon.\n\nStudent: Do I need to submit anything else?\n\nProfessor: No. Once I approve it, the system should let you enroll.","questions":[{"n":11,"question":"Why could the student not enroll immediately?","options":{"A":"The course was already full.","B":"Registration initially gives priority to students in another program.","C":"Economics students are not allowed to take the course.","D":"The professor had not activated the course."},"answer":"B"},{"n":12,"question":"What will probably happen next?","options":{"A":"The student will change majors.","B":"The student will submit another application.","C":"The professor will authorize the student to enroll.","D":"The course will be moved to another semester."},"answer":"C"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m1-02-conversation-2.mp3"},{"kind":"announcement","title":"Announcement","transcript":"The east entrance to the university museum will close tomorrow while workers replace several exterior doors. Visitors should enter through the courtyard entrance on Franklin Street instead. The museum's opening hours will not change, and all galleries will remain accessible. However, the bicycle racks beside the east entrance will also be unavailable, so visitors arriving by bicycle should use the racks behind the library.","questions":[{"n":13,"question":"What is the purpose of the announcement?","options":{"A":"To announce changes caused by construction work","B":"To introduce new museum opening hours","C":"To explain why several galleries are closed","D":"To encourage visitors to use bicycles"},"answer":"A"},{"n":14,"question":"What should visitors arriving by bicycle do?","options":{"A":"Enter through the east entrance","B":"Leave their bicycles near the library","C":"Use the museum courtyard for parking","D":"Return after the construction has finished"},"answer":"B"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m1-03-announcement.mp3"},{"kind":"academic-talk","title":"Academic Talk","transcript":"### Isostatic Rebound\n\nWhen a large ice sheet forms over land, its enormous weight pushes the Earth's crust downward. During the last ice age, thick ice covered large portions of northern Europe and North America, depressing the crust beneath it.\n\nWhen the climate warmed and the ice melted, that weight disappeared. The crust did not immediately return to its previous position, however. The material beneath the crust behaves very slowly, so the land has been rising gradually for thousands of years. This process is called isostatic rebound.\n\nThe effect continues today. In parts of Scandinavia, for example, land is still rising by several millimeters each year. This movement can alter coastlines: areas that were once shallow sea floor may eventually become dry land.\n\nScientists can measure this uplift with satellite positioning systems. These measurements provide information not only about past ice sheets but also about how material deep inside the Earth responds to pressure.","questions":[{"n":15,"question":"What is the talk mainly about?","options":{"A":"How glaciers form in northern Europe","B":"Why some land continues rising after ancient ice sheets disappear","C":"How coastlines cause changes in Earth's crust","D":"Why satellite measurements underestimate sea-level change"},"answer":"B"},{"n":16,"question":"What happened to the crust beneath large ice sheets?","options":{"A":"It moved upward because the ice froze.","B":"It was pushed downward by the ice's weight.","C":"It became permanently thinner.","D":"It moved toward the coast."},"answer":"B"},{"n":17,"question":"Why does the land continue to rise long after the ice has melted?","options":{"A":"Material beneath the crust responds slowly to the removal of pressure.","B":"Modern glaciers continue adding material to the crust.","C":"Sea level pushes the land upward.","D":"Satellite systems produce delayed measurements."},"answer":"A"},{"n":18,"question":"Why does the speaker mention satellite positioning systems?","options":{"A":"To explain how scientists measure ongoing crustal movement","B":"To show that ancient ice sheets can be observed directly","C":"To compare Scandinavian coastlines with North American ones","D":"To demonstrate why sea-floor elevation cannot be measured"},"answer":"A"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m1-04-academic-talk.mp3"}]},{"choose":[{"n":1,"prompt":"Man: Didn't the bookstore say your order would be ready today?","options":{"A":"They did, but one of the books hasn't arrived.","B":"I ordered lunch instead.","C":"The bookstore closes at six.","D":"Yes, the book was interesting."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m2-response-01.mp3"},{"n":2,"prompt":"Woman: What made you choose that apartment?","options":{"A":"It's within walking distance of campus.","B":"I chose three courses.","C":"The apartment was chosen yesterday.","D":"I haven't made anything yet."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m2-response-02.mp3"},{"n":3,"prompt":"Man: I wonder whether Professor Allen received my email.","options":{"A":"You could ask her after class.","B":"Her email address is very short.","C":"I received the package yesterday.","D":"The professor wonders about that too."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m2-response-03.mp3"},{"n":4,"prompt":"Woman: Aren't you supposed to be at the training session?","options":{"A":"It doesn't start for another hour.","B":"The trainer was very helpful.","C":"Yes, I suppose the room is large.","D":"I attended the session yesterday."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m2-response-04.mp3"},{"n":5,"prompt":"Man: Would it be possible to move our appointment to Thursday?","options":{"A":"Thursday should work for me.","B":"I moved here last year.","C":"The appointment was very useful.","D":"It's possible to walk there."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m2-response-05.mp3"},{"n":6,"prompt":"Woman: Why don't we take the earlier train?","options":{"A":"We'd have to leave almost immediately.","B":"The train station is downtown.","C":"I took it last month.","D":"Yes, it arrived early."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m2-response-06.mp3"},{"n":7,"prompt":"Man: Have you figured out why the calculation is different?","options":{"A":"I think I used the wrong value in the first step.","B":"The calculator belongs to Maria.","C":"It's different from the blue one.","D":"I calculated the distance yesterday."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m2-response-07.mp3"},{"n":8,"prompt":"Woman: You haven't registered for the workshop yet?","options":{"A":"I was waiting to confirm my schedule.","B":"Registration is beside the entrance.","C":"The workshop lasted all afternoon.","D":"No, the register isn't working."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m2-response-08.mp3"}],"groups":[{"kind":"conversation","title":"Conversation","transcript":"Student: Hi, I'm supposed to volunteer at Saturday's orientation, but my assignment says “north check-in,” and I'm not sure where that is.\n\nCoordinator: That's the desk outside North Hall. You'll be checking students' names as they arrive.\n\nStudent: Okay. I thought I might be giving campus tours.\n\nCoordinator: That was the original plan, but we had more tour volunteers than we needed. If things get busy later, we may move you to the information table.","questions":[{"n":9,"question":"Why does the student speak to the coordinator?","options":{"A":"To find out where to report for a volunteer assignment","B":"To request permission to miss orientation","C":"To volunteer for an additional shift","D":"To ask how to register for a campus tour"},"answer":"A"},{"n":10,"question":"What does the coordinator say about the student's assignment?","options":{"A":"It will definitely change later.","B":"It was changed because too few tour volunteers registered.","C":"The student may be given another task if needed.","D":"The student will lead campus tours in the afternoon."},"answer":"C"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m2-01-conversation.mp3"},{"kind":"announcement","title":"Announcement","transcript":"Students interested in applying for the Westbrook Research Scholarship are invited to a short application workshop Wednesday at 4:00 p.m. in Library Room 106. The workshop will focus specifically on the required project statement. General questions about financial eligibility will not be covered; those should be directed to the Financial Aid Office. Attendance is optional and will not affect scholarship decisions. Students should bring a preliminary project idea if they would like feedback during the session.","questions":[{"n":11,"question":"What will the workshop mainly address?","options":{"A":"Whether students qualify financially","B":"How scholarship applications are evaluated","C":"One written component of the scholarship application","D":"How research funding is distributed"},"answer":"C"},{"n":12,"question":"What is suggested about students who attend?","options":{"A":"They should already have a complete application.","B":"They may receive feedback if they bring an initial idea.","C":"They will receive preference in scholarship decisions.","D":"They must first visit the Financial Aid Office."},"answer":"B"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m2-02-announcement.mp3"},{"kind":"academic-talk","title":"Academic Talk","transcript":"### Semantic Bleaching\n\nWords sometimes become less specific in meaning as they are repeatedly used in new contexts. Linguists call this process semantic bleaching.\n\nConsider the English verb go. Its original meaning involves physical movement, as in “I go to the library.” But in the expression “I'm going to study,” the phrase can indicate a future intention even when no movement occurs. The movement meaning has become weakened.\n\nA similar process occurs with intensifiers. The word awfully once strongly suggested something negative or unpleasant. Yet a speaker can say that a person is “awfully kind,” where the word primarily increases the strength of kind rather than describing anything terrible.\n\nSemantic bleaching does not mean that a word loses all meaning. Instead, part of its original meaning becomes less prominent while a grammatical or discourse function becomes more important. Over long periods, this process can contribute to the development of new grammatical constructions.","questions":[{"n":13,"question":"What is the talk mainly about?","options":{"A":"Why words disappear from languages","B":"How a word's original meaning can weaken as its use changes","C":"Why negative words are more common than positive words","D":"How speakers deliberately invent grammatical rules"},"answer":"B"},{"n":14,"question":"Why does the speaker discuss the verb go?","options":{"A":"To show how a movement word can develop a grammatical function","B":"To demonstrate that verbs change more quickly than adjectives","C":"To explain why future actions require physical movement","D":"To compare English with another language"},"answer":"A"},{"n":15,"question":"What does the example “awfully kind” illustrate?","options":{"A":"A word retaining only its original negative meaning","B":"A word being used incorrectly by modern speakers","C":"An expression in which an older meaning has become less prominent","D":"A phrase that has recently disappeared from English"},"answer":"C"},{"n":16,"question":"What does the speaker say can result from semantic bleaching over time?","options":{"A":"The disappearance of grammatical constructions","B":"The development of new grammatical patterns","C":"The elimination of ambiguous meanings","D":"Greater similarity among unrelated languages"},"answer":"B"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-02/listening/m2-03-academic-talk.mp3"}]}],"writing":{"build":[{"n":1,"question":"Why didn't Lena attend the guest lecture?","prefix":"She","suffix":".","tokens":["hadn't","realized","that","the location","had been","changed"],"correctOrder":["hadn't","realized","that","the location","had been","changed"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"She hadn't realized that the location had been changed."},{"n":2,"question":"Have you decided which statistics course to take?","prefix":"I'm still trying to determine","suffix":".","tokens":["which one","would","be","most useful","for my research"],"correctOrder":["which one","would","be","most useful","for my research"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"I'm still trying to determine which one would be most useful for my research."},{"n":3,"question":"Can the samples be analyzed today?","prefix":"Only if","suffix":".","tokens":["the laboratory","has finished","calibrating","the new","equipment"],"correctOrder":["the laboratory","has finished","calibrating","the new","equipment"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"Only if the laboratory has finished calibrating the new equipment."},{"n":4,"question":"Was the article difficult to understand?","prefix":"It was","suffix":".","tokens":["considerably more","technical","than","I","had","anticipated","rarely"],"correctOrder":["considerably more","technical","than","I","had","anticipated"],"unused":["rarely"],"correctSentence":"It was considerably more technical than I had anticipated."},{"n":5,"question":"Why did Omar revise the questionnaire?","prefix":"He discovered","suffix":".","tokens":["that","several questions","could have been","interpreted","in","different ways"],"correctOrder":["that","several questions","could have been","interpreted","in","different ways"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"He discovered that several questions could have been interpreted in different ways."},{"n":6,"question":"Did the researchers confirm the original explanation?","prefix":"No, although","suffix":".","tokens":["some of","the initial evidence","remained","consistent","with","it"],"correctOrder":["some of","the initial evidence","remained","consistent","with","it"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"No, although some of the initial evidence remained consistent with it."},{"n":7,"question":"Why was the recommendation rejected?","prefix":"It appeared","suffix":".","tokens":["to be","based","on","assumptions","that were","insufficiently supported"],"correctOrder":["to be","based","on","assumptions","that were","insufficiently supported"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"It appeared to be based on assumptions that were insufficiently supported."},{"n":8,"question":"When did the error become obvious?","prefix":"Not until","suffix":".","tokens":["the calculations","were repeated","did","the discrepancy","become apparent"],"correctOrder":["the calculations","were repeated","did","the discrepancy","become apparent"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"Not until the calculations were repeated did the discrepancy become apparent."},{"n":9,"question":"Will the university continue the pilot program?","prefix":"That depends on","suffix":".","tokens":["whether","the results","remain","favorable","next semester"],"correctOrder":["whether","the results","remain","favorable","next semester"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"That depends on whether the results remain favorable next semester."},{"n":10,"question":"How did Nina find the internship?","prefix":"She contacted","suffix":".","tokens":["a researcher","whom","her professor","had","recommended","her"],"correctOrder":["a researcher","whom","her professor","had","recommended"],"unused":["her"],"correctSentence":"She contacted a researcher whom her professor had recommended."}],"email":"Time: 7 minutes\n\nYou registered for a university workshop on digital mapping because the registration page stated that participants would receive temporary access to specialized mapping software.\n\nToday, you learned that the temporary licenses will not be available. Participants may still attend, but they will need access to their own mapping software to complete several activities.\n\nWrite an email to Dr. Maya Chen, the workshop coordinator.\n\nIn your email:\n\n- Explain why you registered for the workshop.\n- Describe the problem caused by the change.\n- Ask for a solution or propose an alternative.\n\nWrite as much as you can in complete sentences.\n\nTo: Dr. Maya Chen\nSubject: Digital Mapping Workshop","discussion":"Time: 10 minutes\n\n### Professor Ibrahim\n\nSome universities allow students to replace one unsuccessful course grade by retaking the course. The original grade may remain visible on the academic record, but only the newer grade is used when calculating the student's grade-point average.\n\nSupporters argue that this policy allows students to demonstrate improvement. Critics argue that students who can afford to repeat courses may gain an advantage over those who cannot.\n\nShould universities allow students to replace unsuccessful grades by retaking courses? Why or why not?\n\n### Talia\n\nI support grade replacement because one poor semester should not permanently affect a student's academic record. If students later demonstrate that they understand the material, the newer grade may represent their ability more accurately.\n\n### Noah\n\nI disagree because students do not all have the same opportunity to repeat courses. Retaking a class requires time and sometimes additional tuition, so grade replacement could benefit students with more financial flexibility.\n\n### Your Response\n\nWrite at least 100 words."},"speaking":{"repeat":{"scenario":"You are learning how to assist visitors at a university botanical garden. Listen to your supervisor and repeat exactly what she says. Repeat only once.","prompts":["Welcome to the botanical garden.","The main greenhouse is straight ahead.","Guided walks begin beside the visitor center.","Please remind visitors to remain on the marked paths.","The tropical greenhouse closes earlier than the outdoor gardens.","If visitors need assistance, direct them to the information desk beside the central fountain.","Before entering the research greenhouse, visitors must check in with a staff member and leave all food and drinks outside."],"audio":["/media/audio/sim-02/speaking/repeat-01.mp3","/media/audio/sim-02/speaking/repeat-02.mp3","/media/audio/sim-02/speaking/repeat-03.mp3","/media/audio/sim-02/speaking/repeat-04.mp3","/media/audio/sim-02/speaking/repeat-05.mp3","/media/audio/sim-02/speaking/repeat-06.mp3","/media/audio/sim-02/speaking/repeat-07.mp3"]},"interview":{"scenario":"You are participating in a university study about how people manage information and make everyday decisions.","questions":["When you need to remember something important, what method do you usually use?","Some people prefer to make decisions quickly, while others prefer to spend more time comparing alternatives. Which approach do you usually prefer, and why?","Imagine your university could provide students with either more digital organization tools or more workshops on time management. Which would be more useful?","Technology can make it easier to store information without memorizing it. In what ways could this benefit students, and what disadvantage might result from relying on it too heavily?"],"audio":["/media/audio/sim-02/speaking/interview-01.mp3","/media/audio/sim-02/speaking/interview-02.mp3","/media/audio/sim-02/speaking/interview-03.mp3","/media/audio/sim-02/speaking/interview-04.mp3"]}}},{"id":3,"name":"Full Simulation 03","reading":[{"cloze":{"text":"Many microorganisms live in communities called biofilms. Instead of floa___ freely, cells att___ to a surface and produce substances that help hold the community together. This structure can prot___ microorganisms from environmental stress and make some chemical treatments less eff___. Biofilms commonly form on rocks, medical devices, and industrial equipment. Their development is not random: cells can det___ nearby conditions and alter gene activity in response. Researchers therefore stu___ biofilms not merely as collections of individual cells but as org___ systems whose behavior emerges from interactions among their members. Understanding these interactions may imp___ methods for controlling harmful biofilms while pres___ those that perform useful ecological functions. Scientists also inv___ how useful biofilms can be encouraged.","answers":[{"missing":"ting","word":"floating"},{"missing":"ach","word":"attach"},{"missing":"ect","word":"protect"},{"missing":"ective","word":"effective"},{"missing":"ect","word":"detect"},{"missing":"dy","word":"study"},{"missing":"anized","word":"organized"},{"missing":"rove","word":"improve"},{"missing":"erve","word":"preserve"},{"missing":"estigate","word":"investigate"}]},"groups":[{"kind":"daily","questions":[{"n":11,"question":"What service will remain available on the second floor?","options":{"A":"Color printing","B":"Software installation","C":"Black-and-white printing","D":"Media equipment rental"},"answer":"C"},{"n":12,"question":"What must students do if they need color printing?","options":{"A":"Send their documents directly to another printer.","B":"Wait until Thursday evening.","C":"Pay an additional printing charge.","D":"Ask a staff member to transfer their documents."},"answer":"A"}],"stimulus":"### CAMPUS PRINTING SERVICE\n\nTemporary Color-Printing Change\n\nColor printers on the second floor will be unavailable Thursday while new software is installed.\n\nBlack-and-white printing will continue normally.\n\nStudents who need color printing may use the machines in the Media Center on the first floor. Charges will remain the same, but documents sent to second-floor printers will not automatically transfer to the Media Center."},{"kind":"daily","questions":[{"n":13,"question":"Why will Amira and Jon not meet at the café?","options":{"A":"It is too far from the library.","B":"It will close earlier than usual.","C":"Jon cannot arrive before 6:00.","D":"A group room has been reserved there."},"answer":"B"},{"n":14,"question":"Why do they reject the library group rooms?","options":{"A":"The library is closed.","B":"The rooms are too small.","C":"No rooms are available.","D":"They cannot bring food inside."},"answer":"C"},{"n":15,"question":"What will Jon probably do?","options":{"A":"Arrive before the planned meeting time","B":"Reserve a room near the language center","C":"Attend the private event first","D":"Meet Amira after 6:00"},"answer":"A"}],"stimulus":"Read a text-message exchange.\n\nAmira: Are we still meeting at the café at 6?\n\nJon: The café closes early tonight because of a private event.\n\nAmira: Oh. Library instead?\n\nJon: Could work, but the group rooms are all reserved.\n\nAmira: There's a seating area outside the language center.\n\nJon: Perfect. I'll get there around 5:50 so we can start on time."},{"kind":"academic","questions":[{"n":16,"question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":{"A":"Why some seeds cannot survive cold temperatures","B":"How seed dormancy helps regulate the timing of germination","C":"Why rainfall prevents seeds from growing","D":"How young plants respond to fire"},"answer":"B"},{"n":17,"question":"Why does the author mention warm weather during winter?","options":{"A":"To illustrate why immediate germination can sometimes be harmful","B":"To show that all seeds require freezing temperatures","C":"To explain how rainfall affects plant growth","D":"To identify the primary cause of dormancy"},"answer":"A"},{"n":18,"question":"The word “premature” is closest in meaning to","options":{"A":"unusually slow","B":"occurring too early","C":"biologically impossible","D":"highly successful"},"answer":"B"},{"n":19,"question":"Which of the following can trigger germination in some species?","options":{"A":"Exposure to environmental signals such as fire or rainfall","B":"Complete absence of temperature change","C":"Competition with mature plants","D":"Permanent darkness"},"answer":"A"},{"n":20,"question":"What does the final paragraph emphasize?","options":{"A":"Dormancy controls the timing rather than merely the speed of growth.","B":"All plants rely on the same environmental signal.","C":"Dormancy usually disappears after one season.","D":"Germination is independent of environmental history."},"answer":"A"}],"stimulus":"### Seed Dormancy\n\nA seed may remain alive without germinating even when moisture and temperature initially appear favorable. This temporary state, known as dormancy, prevents germination until particular environmental or physiological conditions are satisfied.\n\nDormancy can be advantageous because conditions suitable for germination are not necessarily suitable for the survival of a young plant. A brief period of warm weather during winter, for example, could stimulate premature growth that would later be destroyed by freezing temperatures. Some seeds therefore require prolonged exposure to cold before they become capable of germinating.\n\nOther species respond to fire, changes in light, or chemicals released after rainfall. These mechanisms vary considerably, but they serve a similar function: they connect germination to environmental signals that historically predicted favorable growing conditions. Dormancy should therefore not be interpreted simply as delayed growth. It is a mechanism through which plants regulate when growth begins."}]},{"cloze":{"text":"Maps appear to represent geographic space objectively, but every map invol___ selection. A cartographer must dec___ which features to include, how prominently to display them, and what scale to use. These choices can aff___ interpretation. A transportation map, for example, may dist___ physical distances in order to make routes easier to follow. Political maps may emph___ national boundaries while omitting other forms of social or cultural organization. None of this necessarily makes a map inaccur___; rather, it reflects the fact that maps are designed for particular pur___. Understanding a map therefore req___ attention not only to what it shows but also to what it leaves out. The absence of information may be as sig___ as its visible pres___.","answers":[{"missing":"ves","word":"involves"},{"missing":"ide","word":"decide"},{"missing":"ect","word":"affect"},{"missing":"ort","word":"distort"},{"missing":"asize","word":"emphasize"},{"missing":"ate","word":"inaccurate"},{"missing":"poses","word":"purposes"},{"missing":"uires","word":"requires"},{"missing":"nificant","word":"significant"},{"missing":"ence","word":"presence"}]},"groups":[{"kind":"daily","questions":[{"n":11,"question":"What will happen during the replacement work?","options":{"A":"All changing-room lockers will close at once.","B":"Some lockers will remain available throughout the project.","C":"Pool lockers will be removed permanently.","D":"Semester locker rentals will automatically end."},"answer":"B"},{"n":12,"question":"Who will receive an email?","options":{"A":"Every recreation-center member","B":"People using day-use lockers","C":"Members whose rented lockers are affected","D":"Students who use the swimming pool"},"answer":"C"}],"stimulus":"### RECREATION CENTER\n\nLocker Replacement\n\nBeginning next week, lockers in the men's and women's changing rooms will be replaced in stages.\n\nDuring the work, approximately one-third of the lockers will be unavailable at any given time. Day-use lockers near the swimming pool will remain open.\n\nMembers who currently rent a locker for the semester will receive an email if their specific locker is affected."},{"kind":"daily","questions":[{"n":13,"question":"What has changed about the tour?","options":{"A":"Its starting time","B":"Its meeting location","C":"Its route","D":"Its ticket price"},"answer":"C"},{"n":14,"question":"Why was the change made?","options":{"A":"Construction has closed part of the route.","B":"Hot weather is expected.","C":"The museum has changed its hours.","D":"Fewer participants registered."},"answer":"B"},{"n":15,"question":"What can participants with museum tickets do?","options":{"A":"Visit the museum separately after the tour","B":"Exchange the tickets for bus passes","C":"Use them only at Riverside Plaza","D":"Request a refund before Saturday"},"answer":"A"}],"stimulus":"Read an email.\n\nTo: participants@cityhistory.org\nFrom: programs@cityhistory.org\nSubject: Saturday Walking Tour\n\nHello,\n\nSaturday's architectural walking tour will begin at 10:30 a.m., as scheduled.\n\nBecause the forecast calls for unusually high temperatures, however, the route has been shortened. The tour will now finish at Riverside Plaza rather than continuing to the Old Railway District.\n\nParticipants who purchased the optional Railway District museum ticket may still use it independently later that day. The museum is approximately fifteen minutes from Riverside Plaza by bus.\n\nPlease bring water and wear comfortable shoes.\n\nCity History Programs"},{"kind":"academic","questions":[{"n":16,"question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":{"A":"Why rural areas experience greater temperature variation","B":"Factors that contribute to temperature differences in cities","C":"Why concrete prevents cities from cooling during the day","D":"How cities eliminate vegetation"},"answer":"B"},{"n":17,"question":"Why are cities often warmer after sunset?","options":{"A":"Buildings produce additional sunlight.","B":"Certain urban materials gradually release stored heat.","C":"Plants stop releasing water permanently.","D":"Rural areas receive less solar energy."},"answer":"B"},{"n":18,"question":"What role does vegetation play according to the passage?","options":{"A":"It can help lower surrounding temperatures.","B":"It increases the amount of heat absorbed by pavement.","C":"It prevents all temperature differences within cities.","D":"It releases heat during the night."},"answer":"A"},{"n":19,"question":"Why do researchers compare neighborhoods within the same city?","options":{"A":"Urban temperatures can vary substantially from one neighborhood to another.","B":"Metropolitan boundaries change frequently.","C":"Rural temperatures cannot be measured accurately.","D":"Parking lots are found only in certain cities."},"answer":"A"},{"n":20,"question":"What can be inferred from the final paragraph?","options":{"A":"All residents of a city face approximately the same heat exposure.","B":"Neighborhood characteristics may influence residents' heat-related risk.","C":"Tree cover matters only in rural environments.","D":"Heat islands affect health but not temperature."},"answer":"B"}],"stimulus":"### Urban Heat Islands\n\nCities are often warmer than surrounding rural areas, particularly at night. This difference is known as the urban heat-island effect.\n\nOne reason is that materials such as asphalt and concrete absorb large amounts of solar energy during the day and release it gradually after sunset. Vegetation produces a different effect. Plants cool their surroundings both by providing shade and by releasing water vapor, so replacing vegetation with buildings and pavement can reduce natural cooling.\n\nThe strength of an urban heat island is not uniform across an entire city. Neighborhoods with dense tree cover may remain considerably cooler than areas dominated by dark roofs and parking lots. Consequently, researchers increasingly study temperature differences within cities rather than treating each city as a single thermal environment. Such variation is important because exposure to extreme heat—and the health risks associated with it—may differ substantially even among residents of the same metropolitan area."}]}],"listening":[{"choose":[{"n":1,"prompt":"Woman: Didn't Carlos say he would bring the projector?","options":{"A":"He did, but he's running late.","B":"The projection was very clear.","C":"Carlos brought lunch yesterday.","D":"Yes, the projector is expensive."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m1-response-01.mp3"},{"n":2,"prompt":"Man: Do you know whether the library stays open during the holiday?","options":{"A":"I think the hours are reduced.","B":"The library is across the street.","C":"The holiday was last month.","D":"I stayed there until nine."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m1-response-02.mp3"},{"n":3,"prompt":"Woman: Why are you rewriting the introduction?","options":{"A":"My professor said the argument wasn't clear enough.","B":"I introduced myself yesterday.","C":"The writing center is upstairs.","D":"Yes, the introduction was first."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m1-response-03.mp3"},{"n":4,"prompt":"Man: I thought you had already submitted the application.","options":{"A":"So did I, but apparently it didn't go through.","B":"The application is fairly short.","C":"I applied for two programs.","D":"Yes, the deadline is Friday."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m1-response-04.mp3"},{"n":5,"prompt":"Woman: Could you give me a hand moving these boxes?","options":{"A":"Sure. Where do they need to go?","B":"I have two hands.","C":"The boxes arrived this morning.","D":"They're made of cardboard."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m1-response-05.mp3"},{"n":6,"prompt":"Man: How was the workshop?","options":{"A":"More useful than I expected.","B":"It was in Room 205.","C":"About twenty people.","D":"I worked until five."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m1-response-06.mp3"},{"n":7,"prompt":"Woman: You wouldn't know who borrowed this camera, would you?","options":{"A":"You could check the equipment log.","B":"The camera takes good pictures.","C":"I borrowed a book yesterday.","D":"Yes, I wouldn't."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m1-response-07.mp3"},{"n":8,"prompt":"Man: Should we wait until Priya gets here?","options":{"A":"Probably, since she has the final data.","B":"Priya waited outside.","C":"The data were collected last month.","D":"I got here around ten."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m1-response-08.mp3"}],"groups":[{"kind":"conversation-1","title":"Conversation 1","transcript":"Student: Hi. I'm here to pick up the keys for the rehearsal room I reserved.\n\nEmployee: What's your reservation time?\n\nStudent: Three o'clock.\n\nEmployee: Okay, you're a little early. We can't release the key until fifteen minutes before the reservation because another group is still using the room.\n\nStudent: That's fine. Can I wait here?\n\nEmployee: Sure. They usually return the key on time.","questions":[{"n":9,"question":"Why can the student not receive the key yet?","options":{"A":"The reservation has been canceled.","B":"Another group is still using the room.","C":"The student arrived after the reservation time.","D":"The employee cannot find the reservation."},"answer":"B"},{"n":10,"question":"What will the student probably do?","options":{"A":"Reserve another room","B":"Wait until the key can be released","C":"Join the group currently rehearsing","D":"Return the following day"},"answer":"B"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m1-01-conversation-1.mp3"},{"kind":"conversation-2","title":"Conversation 2","transcript":"Student: Professor Blake, I noticed you marked my report as incomplete, but I uploaded the appendix separately.\n\nProfessor: Ah, that may explain it. I downloaded only the main file.\n\nStudent: Should I upload everything again as one document?\n\nProfessor: Yes, please. I haven't graded the report yet, so if you combine the files today, there won't be any penalty.","questions":[{"n":11,"question":"Why was the student's report marked incomplete?","options":{"A":"The student submitted it after the deadline.","B":"The professor did not see one part of the submission.","C":"The appendix contained incorrect information.","D":"The main file could not be opened."},"answer":"B"},{"n":12,"question":"What does the professor ask the student to do?","options":{"A":"Rewrite the appendix","B":"Submit the report as one combined file","C":"Explain why the report was late","D":"Wait until grading is complete"},"answer":"B"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m1-02-conversation-2.mp3"},{"kind":"announcement","title":"Announcement","transcript":"Students attending tonight's astronomy observation should meet outside the Physics Building at 8:15 rather than going directly to the observatory. A university shuttle will leave from there at 8:30. Students who drive to the observatory independently should be aware that the upper parking area is closed for resurfacing, so only limited parking is available near the entrance. The observation itself will proceed unless cloud cover becomes too heavy. Any cancellation will be announced by 7:00 p.m.","questions":[{"n":13,"question":"Where should students taking university transportation meet?","options":{"A":"At the observatory entrance","B":"Outside the Physics Building","C":"In the upper parking area","D":"At the astronomy department office"},"answer":"B"},{"n":14,"question":"What may cause the observation to be canceled?","options":{"A":"Road construction","B":"Lack of parking","C":"Cloudy conditions","D":"A shortage of shuttle seats"},"answer":"C"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m1-03-announcement.mp3"},{"kind":"academic-talk","title":"Academic Talk","transcript":"### Cooperative Breeding\n\nIn most bird species, parents raise their own offspring without assistance from other adults. But in some species, additional individuals help feed or protect young birds even though those young are not their own offspring. This behavior is known as cooperative breeding.\n\nWhy would a bird spend energy helping another pair reproduce? One explanation involves genetic relatedness. Helpers are often older offspring of the breeding pair, so assisting younger siblings can indirectly promote the survival of genes they share.\n\nBut relatedness is not the whole explanation. In some species, helpers may eventually inherit access to territory or gain experience that improves their own reproductive success later. Environmental conditions also matter. When suitable breeding territory is scarce, young adults may have little opportunity to establish territories of their own and may remain with their parents instead.\n\nCooperative breeding therefore illustrates how a behavior that appears individually costly can persist when it provides indirect or delayed benefits.","questions":[{"n":15,"question":"What is the talk mainly about?","options":{"A":"Why young birds usually leave their parents","B":"Possible explanations for cooperative breeding","C":"Why birds compete for food within families","D":"How breeding territories are created"},"answer":"B"},{"n":16,"question":"Why might genetic relatedness encourage helping behavior?","options":{"A":"Helpers may contribute to the survival of relatives with shared genes.","B":"Related birds always occupy separate territories.","C":"Helpers become the biological parents of younger birds.","D":"Genetic similarity reduces the need for food."},"answer":"A"},{"n":17,"question":"What is one possible direct benefit for helpers?","options":{"A":"They may gain useful experience.","B":"They immediately produce more offspring.","C":"They avoid interacting with other birds.","D":"They eliminate competition for territory."},"answer":"A"},{"n":18,"question":"Why does the speaker mention scarce breeding territory?","options":{"A":"To explain why some young adults may remain with their parents","B":"To show that cooperative breeding occurs only in small populations","C":"To argue that breeding success depends entirely on territory size","D":"To explain why parents abandon their offspring"},"answer":"A"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m1-04-academic-talk.mp3"}]},{"choose":[{"n":1,"prompt":"Man: Haven't they announced the scholarship results yet?","options":{"A":"Not that I've seen.","B":"The announcement was very loud.","C":"I applied last semester.","D":"Yes, scholarships are helpful."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m2-response-01.mp3"},{"n":2,"prompt":"Woman: Why don't you take the later flight?","options":{"A":"Then I'd miss the first day of the conference.","B":"The airport is about an hour away.","C":"I flew there last year.","D":"The conference lasts three days."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m2-response-02.mp3"},{"n":3,"prompt":"Man: Could you remind me where the meeting is?","options":{"A":"Room 412, I think.","B":"I remembered the meeting.","C":"It starts around four.","D":"Yes, I'll remind you tomorrow."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m2-response-03.mp3"},{"n":4,"prompt":"Woman: I can't figure out why this graph looks different.","options":{"A":"Maybe the axis scale changed.","B":"The graph is in the report.","C":"I figured it out yesterday.","D":"It looks very professional."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m2-response-04.mp3"},{"n":5,"prompt":"Man: Wasn't the guest speaker supposed to arrive this morning?","options":{"A":"Her train was delayed.","B":"The speaker system works now.","C":"She spoke about transportation.","D":"Morning classes are busy."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m2-response-05.mp3"},{"n":6,"prompt":"Woman: Would you mind sending me the notes from yesterday?","options":{"A":"Sure, I'll email them tonight.","B":"Yesterday was exhausting.","C":"I took the train home.","D":"No, the notes weren't difficult."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m2-response-06.mp3"},{"n":7,"prompt":"Man: How come the cafeteria is so crowded?","options":{"A":"The other dining hall is closed today.","B":"I usually eat around noon.","C":"The cafeteria serves breakfast.","D":"The crowd left earlier."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m2-response-07.mp3"},{"n":8,"prompt":"Woman: You haven't decided whether you're going?","options":{"A":"I'm still waiting to hear about my work schedule.","B":"I decided that yesterday was better.","C":"The event is in the auditorium.","D":"Yes, I haven't gone."},"answer":"A","audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m2-response-08.mp3"}],"groups":[{"kind":"conversation","title":"Conversation","transcript":"Student: Hi. I signed up for tomorrow's career advising appointment, but I just realized I selected “graduate school” instead of “employment.”\n\nAdvisor: That's not a problem. The appointment length is the same.\n\nStudent: So I don't have to make another reservation?\n\nAdvisor: No. I'll change the topic in the system. Just bring your résumé if you want feedback on it.","questions":[{"n":9,"question":"Why does the student speak with the advisor?","options":{"A":"To correct information in an appointment reservation","B":"To cancel an advising appointment","C":"To extend the length of an appointment","D":"To ask about graduate-school applications"},"answer":"A"},{"n":10,"question":"What does the advisor tell the student?","options":{"A":"A new appointment is required.","B":"The student should bring a résumé if feedback is desired.","C":"Employment appointments are shorter.","D":"The reservation cannot be changed."},"answer":"B"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m2-01-conversation.mp3"},{"kind":"announcement","title":"Announcement","transcript":"This Friday, the student art gallery will close at 4:00 p.m., two hours earlier than usual, while staff prepare for the annual exhibition opening. Students whose artwork has been selected for the exhibition may deliver pieces until 3:30 p.m. through the rear entrance. The opening reception begins at 6:30 and is open to everyone. Artists do not need a separate invitation, but they should check in at the reception desk when they arrive.","questions":[{"n":11,"question":"Why will the gallery close early?","options":{"A":"Staff need time to prepare for an event.","B":"Artwork will be removed from the building.","C":"The rear entrance is being repaired.","D":"The gallery is changing its regular hours permanently."},"answer":"A"},{"n":12,"question":"What are participating artists told to do at the reception?","options":{"A":"Bring another invitation","B":"Check in when they arrive","C":"Deliver artwork after 6:30","D":"Use only the rear entrance"},"answer":"B"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m2-02-announcement.mp3"},{"kind":"academic-talk","title":"Academic Talk","transcript":"### Price Anchoring\n\nImagine that you are considering buying a chair. Before seeing any products, you might have only a vague idea of what a reasonable chair should cost. But suppose the first chair you see costs $1,200. A second chair priced at $500 may suddenly appear inexpensive, even though you might have considered $500 expensive in a different context.\n\nThis illustrates anchoring. An initial number can influence later judgments even when that number does not provide reliable information about the correct answer.\n\nAnchoring has been observed in many settings. People estimating quantities, negotiating prices, or evaluating financial offers may adjust their judgments away from an initial value, but often not enough.\n\nImportantly, simply knowing that anchoring exists does not always eliminate the effect. People may recognize that an initial number is irrelevant while still allowing it to influence their estimate. Researchers therefore study methods that encourage decision-makers to consider alternative reference points before reaching a conclusion.","questions":[{"n":13,"question":"What is the talk mainly about?","options":{"A":"Why expensive products are usually higher quality","B":"How an initial value can influence later judgments","C":"Why consumers refuse to compare prices","D":"How retailers calculate production costs"},"answer":"B"},{"n":14,"question":"Why does the speaker discuss the $1,200 chair?","options":{"A":"To illustrate how an initial price can affect evaluation of another price","B":"To show that consumers always buy cheaper furniture","C":"To demonstrate that chairs are difficult to price accurately","D":"To explain how retailers choose a reference price"},"answer":"A"},{"n":15,"question":"What does the speaker say about adjustment away from an anchor?","options":{"A":"People often adjust, but not sufficiently.","B":"People never recognize that adjustment is necessary.","C":"Adjustment completely eliminates the original influence.","D":"Only financial experts are able to adjust."},"answer":"A"},{"n":16,"question":"What is suggested about knowing that anchoring exists?","options":{"A":"Awareness alone may not completely prevent the effect.","B":"Awareness makes people more dependent on initial values.","C":"Only researchers can recognize anchoring.","D":"It prevents anchoring in financial decisions but not other decisions."},"answer":"A"}],"audio":"/media/audio/sim-03/listening/m2-03-academic-talk.mp3"}]}],"writing":{"build":[{"n":1,"question":"Why did Ava return to the registrar's office?","prefix":"She","suffix":".","tokens":["wanted","to confirm","whether","her request","had been","processed"],"correctOrder":["wanted","to confirm","whether","her request","had been","processed"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"She wanted to confirm whether her request had been processed."},{"n":2,"question":"Have you finished analyzing the survey?","prefix":"Not yet. I'm","suffix":".","tokens":["still trying","to determine","which responses","should be","excluded"],"correctOrder":["still trying","to determine","which responses","should be","excluded"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"I'm still trying to determine which responses should be excluded."},{"n":3,"question":"Can the experiment begin this afternoon?","prefix":"Only if","suffix":".","tokens":["the samples","have been","properly","prepared","beforehand"],"correctOrder":["the samples","have been","properly","prepared","beforehand"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"Only if the samples have been properly prepared beforehand."},{"n":4,"question":"Was the revised explanation convincing?","prefix":"It was","suffix":".","tokens":["far more","plausible","than","the original one","had","been","completely"],"correctOrder":["far more","plausible","than","the original one","had","been"],"unused":["completely"],"correctSentence":"It was far more plausible than the original one had been."},{"n":5,"question":"Why did the researchers repeat the measurement?","prefix":"They suspected","suffix":".","tokens":["that","the first result","might have been","affected","by","equipment error"],"correctOrder":["that","the first result","might have been","affected","by","equipment error"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"They suspected that the first result might have been affected by equipment error."},{"n":6,"question":"Did the committee accept the evidence?","prefix":"Yes, although","suffix":".","tokens":["its interpretation","remained","somewhat","difficult","to","justify"],"correctOrder":["its interpretation","remained","somewhat","difficult","to","justify"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"Yes, although its interpretation remained somewhat difficult to justify."},{"n":7,"question":"Why was the prediction inaccurate?","prefix":"It failed","suffix":".","tokens":["to account","for","several factors","that had","changed","unexpectedly"],"correctOrder":["to account","for","several factors","that had","changed","unexpectedly"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"It failed to account for several factors that had changed unexpectedly."},{"n":8,"question":"When did the pattern become noticeable?","prefix":"Only after","suffix":".","tokens":["the observations","were grouped","by age","did","it emerge"],"correctOrder":["the observations","were grouped","by age","did","it emerge"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"Only after the observations were grouped by age did it emerge."},{"n":9,"question":"Will the new procedure become permanent?","prefix":"That depends on","suffix":".","tokens":["whether","it proves","effective","over","time"],"correctOrder":["whether","it proves","effective","over","time"],"unused":[],"correctSentence":"That depends on whether it proves effective over time."},{"n":10,"question":"How did Isabel learn about the research position?","prefix":"A professor","suffix":".","tokens":["with whom","she had","previously","worked","recommended","it","her"],"correctOrder":["with whom","she had","previously","worked","recommended","it"],"unused":["her"],"correctSentence":"A professor with whom she had previously worked recommended it."}],"email":"Time: 7 minutes\n\nYou registered for a weekend training session on data visualization because the program description stated that participants would work in small groups and receive individual feedback from instructors.\n\nYou have now learned that enrollment was much higher than expected. Participants will work in one large group, and individual feedback will no longer be available.\n\nWrite an email to Alex Morgan, the program coordinator.\n\nIn your email:\n\n- Explain why you registered.\n- Describe why the change matters to you.\n- Ask whether another option is available.\n\nWrite as much as you can in complete sentences.\n\nTo: Alex Morgan\nSubject: Data Visualization Training","discussion":"Time: 10 minutes\n\n### Professor Hassan\n\nMany universities require students to complete courses outside their major. Supporters argue that studying several fields produces more broadly educated graduates. Critics argue that these requirements reduce the time students can devote to specialized courses directly related to their career goals.\n\nShould university students be required to take courses outside their major? Why or why not?\n\n### Leah\n\nI support the requirement. Students may enter university with very narrow interests, and courses in other fields can expose them to ideas and skills they would never encounter within their major.\n\n### Ethan\n\nI think students should have more choice. Degree programs already require several years of study, and students may benefit more from taking additional advanced courses related directly to their professional goals.\n\n### Your Response\n\nWrite at least 100 words."},"speaking":{"repeat":{"scenario":"You are learning how to assist visitors at a public history museum. Listen to your supervisor and repeat exactly what he says. Repeat only once.","prompts":["Welcome to the history museum.","Tickets are available at the front desk.","The permanent exhibits begin on the second floor.","Visitors may take photographs unless a sign indicates otherwise.","The guided tour begins beside the large map in the central hall.","If visitors want to store a backpack, direct them to the lockers near the main entrance.","Before visitors enter the manuscript gallery, remind them that flash photography and uncovered drinks are prohibited."],"audio":["/media/audio/sim-03/speaking/repeat-01.mp3","/media/audio/sim-03/speaking/repeat-02.mp3","/media/audio/sim-03/speaking/repeat-03.mp3","/media/audio/sim-03/speaking/repeat-04.mp3","/media/audio/sim-03/speaking/repeat-05.mp3","/media/audio/sim-03/speaking/repeat-06.mp3","/media/audio/sim-03/speaking/repeat-07.mp3"]},"interview":{"scenario":"You are participating in a university study about how people learn unfamiliar subjects.","questions":["When you need to learn something completely new, what do you usually do first?","Do you learn more effectively by reading about a topic or by watching someone explain it? Why?","Imagine you have only one hour to prepare for a difficult class discussion. How would you use that time?","Some people think students learn better when they struggle with difficult material before receiving an explanation. What could be one benefit and one disadvantage of this approach?"],"audio":["/media/audio/sim-03/speaking/interview-01.mp3","/media/audio/sim-03/speaking/interview-02.mp3","/media/audio/sim-03/speaking/interview-03.mp3","/media/audio/sim-03/speaking/interview-04.mp3"]}}}];
